From 0bbead60cad39890929e3381689e7e0df4038e63 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 04:11:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/20] feat: load GGUF and NINF models on big-endian hosts

GGUF is little-endian and the loaders read every header field, metadata
value and tensor payload in host order, so on big-endian hosts (PowerPC
Macs, ppc64 BE, s390x) the magic check failed and, past it, every value
would have been byte-swapped.

- GGUF loader, tokenizer metadata reader and the forge-inspect scanner:
  compare the magic as bytes and read all multi-byte header fields in
  file byte order. The order is taken from the version word (always
  small), so big-endian GGUF files from llama.cpp's
  gguf_convert_endian.py load too, without conversion, on big-endian
  hosts.
- Tensor data: when the file's byte order differs from the host's, make
  the private mapping writable and convert every tensor in place once at
  load (copy-on-write pages, the file is never modified); the tensors
  are then used zero-copy as before. byteswap_tensor_data() knows the
  multi-byte fields of each block type (fp16 scales, the uint16 arrays
  of IQ2_XXS/IQ2_XS/IQ1_S/IQ4_XS) and leaves byte arrays alone; the
  kernels that view byte arrays as words were made byte-order
  independent earlier. Unknown types fail the load instead of producing
  garbage.
- NINF loader: convert the (little-endian) header, tensor table and
  tensor copies on big-endian hosts.

Little-endian hosts with little-endian files take exactly the old path.

tests/test_gguf_byteorder.cpp writes one synthetic model as a little- and
a big-endian GGUF (every metadata type, a tokenizer vocabulary, F32/F16/
BF16 and all 19 quantized types) and checks metadata, tokenizer fields
and per-tensor hashes of the dequantized data against values recorded
on x86-64. It passes on x86-64 and on 32-bit big-endian PowerPC
(qemu-ppc), where the dequantized output of the little-endian file is
bit-identical to x86-64.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
